ring. Native to China, it offers delicate beauty and subtle fragrance when other plants are dormant, making it a valuable addition to woodland gardens and shaded borders.', 'Outdoor', false, false, NULL, true, 'Summer', true, 'Late Winter – Early Spring', 'Slow', 'Medium', '{Well-draining,Loamy,Rich}', 'Central and Eastern China', 200, 200, false, 'https://static.inaturalist.org/photos/14994189/square.jpeg', 'https://static.inaturalist.org/photos/14994189/medium.jpeg', true, 706673, 706673, 'species');
INSERT INTO public.cached_botanical_records (scientific_name, common_name, ideal_min_ph, ideal_max_ph, is_toxic_to_pets, propagation_methods, is_ai_enriched, raw_api_payload, cached_at, toxicity_notes, watering, sunlight, cycle, plant_type, check_depth_description, ideal_humidity_min, ideal_humidity_max, care_difficulty, description, placement, is_tropical, is_toxic_to_humans, human_toxicity_notes, produces_fruit, fruit_season, produces_flowers, flowering_season, growth_rate, maintenance_level, preferred_soil_type, native_region, max_height_cm, max_spread_cm, air_purifying, thumbnail_url, regular_url, thumbnail_fetched, inat_taxon_id, inat_species_id, inat_rank) VALUES ('Dicentra formosa', 'Pacific bleeding heart', 6.0, 7.0, true, '{Division,Seeds}', true, NULL, '2026-06-08 14:53:26.005375+00', 'All parts contain toxic alkaloids; ingestion causes gastroenteritis, cardiac arrhythmias, and tremors in pets.', 'Frequent', '{part_shade,full_shade}', 'Perennial', NULL, 'Keep soil consistently moist but not waterlogged; check at surface level and water when top 1–2 cm feel dry.', 60, 80, 'Intermediate', 'A delicate woodland perennial with finely dissected blue-green foliage and heart-shaped pink or white flowers borne on arching stems, prized for shaded garden borders and naturalised settings.', 'Outdoor', false, true, 'All plant parts contain toxic alkaloids; ingestion causes gastrointestinal upset, dizziness, and cardiac effects. Skin contact may cause dermatitis in sensitive individuals.', true, 'Summer', true, 'Spring – Early Summer', 'Moderate', 'Medium', '{Well-draining,Loamy,Rich,Moisture-retaining}', 'Pacific Northwest North America (Washington, Oregon, Northern California)', 45, 60, false, 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/76022505/square.jpg', 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/76022505/medium.jpg', true, 51048, 51048, 'species');
INSERT INTO public.cached_botanical_records (scientific_name, common_name, ideal_min_ph, ideal_max_ph, is_toxic_to_pets, propagation_methods, is_ai_enriched, raw_api_payload, cached_at, toxicity_notes, watering, sunlight, cycle, plant_type, check_depth_description, ideal_humidity_min, ideal_humidity_max, care_difficulty, description, placement, is_tropical, is_toxic_to_humans, human_toxicity_notes, produces_fruit, fruit_season, produces_flowers, flowering_season, growth_rate, maintenance_level, preferred_soil_type, native_region, max_height_cm, max_spread_cm, air_purifying, thumbnail_url, regular_url, thumbnail_fetched, inat_taxon_id, inat_species_id, inat_rank) VALUES ('Phytolacca americana americana', 'Common american pokeweed', 5.5, 7.5, true, '{Seeds,Division}', true, NULL, '2026-06-08 04:18:51.8809+00', 'All parts, especially roots and berries, contain toxic saponins and oxalates. Ingestion causes severe gastroenteritis, muscle weakness, and potential cardiac arrhythmias in pets. Skin contact may cause irritation.', 'Average', '{full_sun,part_shade}', 'Perennial', NULL, 'Allow top 5 cm of soil to dry between waterings once established; does not tolerate waterlogging.', NULL, NULL, 'Beginner', 'A vigorous North American perennial with tall woody stems, large elliptic leaves, and clusters of small white flowers followed by deep purple-black berries. Naturalized across temperate and subtropical regions; historically used for dyes and traditional medicine but now recognized as highly toxic.', 'Outdoor', false, true, 'All plant parts are toxic. Berries cause severe gastroenteritis, abdominal pain, and potentially life-threatening effects including respiratory paralysis if ingested in quantity. Raw or undercooked foliage causes similar poisoning. Skin contact with crushed plant material may cause dermatitis.', true, 'Late Summer – Autumn', true, 'Summer', 'Fast', 'Low', '{Well-draining,Loamy,Rich}', 'Eastern and Central North America', 150, 90, false, 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/313742426/square.jpg', 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/313742426/medium.jpg', true, 81245, 48599, 'variety');
INSERT INTO public.cached_botanical_records (scientific_name, common_name, ideal_min_ph, ideal_max_ph, is_toxic_to_pets, propagation_methods, is_ai_enriched, raw_api_payload, cached_at, toxicity_notes, watering, sunlight, cycle, plant_type, check_depth_description, ideal_humidity_min, ideal_humidity_max, care_difficulty, description, placement, is_tropical, is_toxic_to_humans, human_toxicity_notes, produces_fruit, fruit_season, produces_flowers, flowering_season, growth_rate, maintenance_level, preferred_soil_type, native_region, max_height_cm, max_spread_cm, air_purifying, thumbnail_url, regular_url, thumbnail_fetched, inat_taxon_id, inat_species_id, inat_rank) VALUES ('Phytolacca americana rigida', 'Maritime pokeweed', 5.5, 7.0, true, '{Seeds,Division}', true, NULL, '2026-06-08 04:18:51.8809+00', 'All parts, especially roots and berries, contain saponins and oxalates. Ingestion causes severe gastroenteritis, cardiac arrhythmias, and potentially fatal outcomes in dogs and cats.', 'Average', '{full_sun,part_shade}', 'Perennial', NULL, 'Allow soil surface to dry between waterings; maritime subspecies prefers well-drained sandy or rocky substrates that should not remain waterlogged.', NULL, NULL, 'Intermediate', 'A herbaceous perennial variant of pokeweed with stiff, upright growth and smaller stature than typical P. americana, bearing white-green flowers and dark purple-black berries; native to coastal and brackish regions and highly tolerant of saline and sandy soils.', 'Outdoor', false, true, 'All parts contain saponins and oxalates; ingestion of roots, berries, or leaves causes severe gastroenteritis, cardiac arrhythmias, and potential systemic toxicity. Even cooked berries retain toxicity. Skin contact with fresh plant material may cause dermatitis.', true, 'Late Summer – Autumn', true, 'Spring – Summer', 'Moderate', 'Low', '{Sandy,Well-draining,Poor}', 'Coastal eastern North America, from Nova Scotia to Florida', 120, 90, false, 'https://static.inaturalist.org/photos/155723574/square.jpg', 'https://static.inaturalist.org/photos/155723574/medium.jpg', true, 242241, 48599, 'variety');
INSERT INTO public.cached_botanical_records (scientific_name, common_name, ideal_min_ph, ideal_max_ph, is_toxic_to_pets, propagation_methods, is_ai_enriched, raw_api_payload, cached_at, toxicity_notes, watering, sunlight, cycle, plant_type, check_depth_description, ideal_humidity_min, ideal_humidity_max, care_difficulty, description, placement, is_tropical, is_toxic_to_humans, human_toxicity_notes, produces_fruit, fruit_season, produces_flowers, flowering_season, growth_rate, maintenance_level, preferred_soil_type, native_region, max_height_cm, max_spread_cm, air_purifying, thumbnail_url, regular_url, thumbnail_fetched, inat_taxon_id, inat_species_id, inat_rank) VALUES ('Phytolacca americana', 'American pokeweed', 6.0, 7.5, true, '{Seeds,Division}', true, NULL, '2026-06-08 04:18:51.8809+00', 'All parts are toxic to pets, especially the berries and roots. Ingestion causes severe gastrointestinal upset, salivation, and potentially cardiac arrhythmias.', 'Average', '{full_sun,part_shade}', 'Perennial', NULL, 'Allow soil to dry moderately between waterings. Check soil 5–7 cm deep; water when dry to touch at that depth.', NULL, NULL, 'Beginner', 'A vigorous native perennial with large coarse leaves and distinctive dark purple berries, historically used in traditional cuisine and medicine. Its rapid growth and striking foliage make it a notable wildflower, though all parts are toxic if improperly prepared.', 'Outdoor', false, true, 'All parts are toxic if improperly prepared. Roots, stems, and uncooked berries contain saponins and oxalates causing severe gastrointestinal irritation, mouth ulceration, and potential systemic toxicity. Young shoots may be safe only after extensive boiling by experienced foragers; raw consumption is dangerous.', true, 'Late Summer – Autumn', true, 'Summer', 'Fast', 'Low', '{Well-draining,Loamy,Rich}', 'Eastern and central North America', 150, 120, false, 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/164206478/square.jpeg', 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/164206478/medium.jpeg', true, 48599, 48599, 'species');
INSERT INTO public.cached_botanical_records (scientific_name, common_name, ideal_min_ph, ideal_max_ph, is_toxic_to_pets, propagation_methods, is_ai_enriched, raw_api_payload, cached_at, toxicity_notes, watering, sunlight, cycle, plant_type, check_depth_description, ideal_humidity_min, ideal_humidity_max, care_difficulty, description, placement, is_tropical, is_toxic_to_humans, human_toxicity_notes, produces_fruit, fruit_season, produces_flowers, flowering_season, growth_rate, maintenance_level, preferred_soil_type, native_region, max_height_cm, max_spread_cm, air_purifying, thumbnail_url, regular_url, thumbnail_fetched, inat_taxon_id, inat_species_id, inat_rank) VALUES ('Bromus tectorum', 'Cheatgrass', 6.0, 8.0, true, '{Seeds}', true, NULL, '2026-06-08 14:53:26.005375+00', 'Awns can lodge in mouth, throat, and digestive tract of livestock and pets; can cause mechanical injury and infection.', 'Minimum', '{full_sun}', 'Annual', NULL, 'This annual grass requires consistent moisture during germination and early establishment. Once established, water only during drought stress. Check soil surface and top 1–2 cm during growing season.', NULL, NULL, 'Beginner', 'A fast-growing annual grass native to the eastern Mediterranean and central Asia, characterized by bristly awns and fine foliage. Now a widespread invasive weed in North American rangelands and disturbed soils, highly competitive and fire-prone.', 'Outdoor', false, false, NULL, true, 'Late Spring – Summer', true, 'Spring – Summer', 'Fast', 'Low', '{Sandy,Loamy,Well-draining,Poor}', 'Eastern Mediterranean and Central Asia', 60, 30, false, 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/58336299/square.jpeg', 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/58336299/medium.jpeg', true, 58369, 58369, 'species');
INSERT INTO public.cached_botanical_records (scientific_name, common_name, ideal_min_ph, ideal_max_ph, is_toxic_to_pets, propagation_methods, is_ai_enriched, raw_api_payload, cached_at, toxicity_notes, watering, sunlight, cycle, plant_type, check_depth_description, ideal_humidity_min, ideal_humidity_max, care_difficulty, description, placement, is_tropical, is_toxic_to_humans, human_toxicity_notes, produces_fruit, fruit_season, produces_flowers, flowering_season, growth_rate, maintenance_level, preferred_soil_type, native_region, max_height_cm, max_spread_cm, air_purifying, thumbnail_url, regular_url, thumbnail_fetched, inat_taxon_id, inat_species_id, inat_rank) VALUES ('Rosa multiflora cathayensis', 'Rosa multiflora cathayensis', 6.0, 7.0, false, '{"Stem Cuttings",Seeds}', true, NULL, '2026-06-08 04:19:49.742762+00', NULL, 'Average', '{full_sun}', 'Perennial', NULL, 'Allow top 2–3 cm of soil to dry between waterings during growing season. In dormancy, reduce frequency significantly.', 40, 60, 'Intermediate', 'A deciduous shrub rose native to East Asia, characterized by clusters of small pink or white flowers and thorny canes. Valued for ornamental landscape use and naturalizing in temperate regions.', 'Outdoor', false, false, NULL, true, 'Autumn', true, 'Spring – Summer', 'Fast', 'Medium', '{Well-draining,Loamy}', 'Eastern Asia, China', 300, 250, false, 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/266187402/square.jpg', 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/266187402/medium.jpg', true, 601016, 78882, 'variety');
INSERT INTO public.cached_botanical_records (scientific_name, common_name, ideal_min_ph, ideal_max_ph, is_toxic_to_pets, propagation_methods, is_ai_enriched, raw_api_payload, cached_at, toxicity_notes, watering, sunlight, cycle, plant_type, check_depth_description, ideal_humidity_min, ideal_humidity_max, care_difficulty, description, placement, is_tropical, is_toxic_to_humans, human_toxicity_notes, produces_fruit, fruit_season, produces_flowers, flowering_season, growth_rate, maintenance_level, preferred_soil_type, native_region, max_height_cm, max_spread_cm, air_purifying, thumbnail_url, regular_url, thumbnail_fetched, inat_taxon_id, inat_species_id, inat_rank) VALUES ('Rosa multiflora', 'Multiflora rose', 6.0, 7.0, false, '{"Stem Cuttings",Seeds}', true, NULL, '2026-06-08 04:19:49.742762+00', NULL, 'Average', '{full_sun}', 'Perennial', NULL, 'Allow top 2–3 cm of soil to dry between waterings during growing season; reduce frequency in winter.', 40, 60, 'Intermediate', 'A vigorous deciduous shrub rose with small, fragrant clustered flowers and dense cane growth, widely cultivated as a rootstock for grafted roses and ornamental hedge. Hardy and prolific, though considered invasive in some regions.', 'Outdoor', false, false, NULL, true, 'Autumn – Winter', true, 'Late Spring – Early Summer', 'Fast', 'Medium', '{Well-draining,Loamy}', 'East Asia (Japan, Korea, China)', 300, 250, false, 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/252786276/square.jpg', 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/252786276/medium.jpg', true, 78882, 78882, 'species');
INSERT INTO public.cached_botanical_records (scientific_name, common_name, ideal_min_ph, ideal_max_ph, is_toxic_to_pets, propagation_methods, is_ai_enriched, raw_api_payload, cached_at, toxicity_notes, watering, sunlight, cycle, plant_type, check_depth_description, ideal_humidity_min, ideal_humidity_max, care_difficulty, description, placement, is_tropical, is_toxic_to_humans, human_toxicity_notes, produces_fruit, fruit_season, produces_flowers, flowering_season, growth_rate, maintenance_level, preferred_soil_type, native_region, max_height_cm, max_spread_cm, air_purifying, thumbnail_url, regular_url, thumbnail_fetched, inat_taxon_id, inat_species_id, inat_rank) VALUES ('Rosa multiflora adenochaeta', 'Rosa multiflora adenochaeta', 6.0, 7.0, false, '{"Stem Cuttings",Seeds}', true, NULL, '2026-06-08 04:19:49.742762+00', NULL, 'Average', '{full_sun,part_shade}', 'Perennial', NULL, 'Water when top 2–3 cm of soil feels dry; ensure soil remains consistently moist but not waterlogged during growing season.', 40, 70, 'Beginner', 'A vigorous deciduous shrub rose with clusters of small white to pale pink flowers and distinctive glandular sepals. Hardy and prolific bloomer, suitable for hedging, rambling, or naturalized plantings.', 'Outdoor', false, false, NULL, true, 'Autumn – Winter', true, 'Spring – Summer', 'Fast', 'Low', '{Well-draining,Loamy,Rich}', 'Eastern Asia (Japan, Korea, China)', 300, 250, false, 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/527950437/square.jpg', 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/527950437/medium.jpg', true, 1498138, 78882, 'variety');
INSERT INTO public.cached_botanical_records (scientific_name, common_name, ideal_min_ph, ideal_max_ph, is_toxic_to_pets, propagation_methods, is_ai_enriched, raw_api_payload, cached_at, toxicity_notes, watering, sunlight, cycle, plant_type, check_depth_description, ideal_humidity_min, ideal_humidity_max, care_difficulty, description, placement, is_tropical, is_toxic_to_humans, human_toxicity_notes, produces_fruit, fruit_season, produces_flowers, flowering_season, growth_rate, maintenance_level, preferred_soil_type, native_region, max_height_cm, max_spread_cm, air_purifying, thumbnail_url, regular_url, thumbnail_fetched, inat_taxon_id, inat_species_id, inat_rank) VALUES ('Rosa multiflora multiflora', 'Rosa multiflora multiflora', 6.0, 6.8, false, '{"Stem Cuttings",Seeds}', true, NULL, '2026-06-08 04:19:49.742762+00', NULL, 'Average', '{full_sun}', 'Perennial', NULL, 'Allow soil to dry slightly between waterings during growing season; water deeply but infrequently to encourage deep root development.', 40, 60, 'Beginner', 'A vigorous, deciduous shrub rose producing abundant clusters of small, fragrant flowers followed by bright red hips. Highly adaptable and cold-hardy, prized for hedging, erosion control, and wildlife habitat.', 'Outdoor', false, false, NULL, true, 'Autumn – Winter', true, 'Late Spring – Summer', 'Fast', 'Low', '{Well-draining,Loamy,Rich}', 'East Asia (Japan, Korea, China)', 300, 400, false, 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/516180054/square.jpg', 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/516180054/medium.jpg', true, 1498139, 78882, 'variety');
INSERT INTO public.cached_botanical_records (scientific_name, common_name, ideal_min_ph, ideal_max_ph, is_toxic_to_pets, propagation_methods, is_ai_enriched, raw_api_payload, cached_at, toxicity_notes, watering, sunlight, cycle, plant_type, check_depth_description, ideal_humidity_min, ideal_humidity_max, care_difficulty, description, placement, is_tropical, is_toxic_to_humans, human_toxicity_notes, produces_fruit, fruit_season, produces_flowers, flowering_season, growth_rate, maintenance_level, preferred_soil_type, native_region, max_height_cm, max_spread_cm, air_purifying, thumbnail_url, regular_url, thumbnail_fetched, inat_taxon_id, inat_species_id, inat_rank) VALUES ('Rosa × rehderiana', 'Polyantha rose', 6.0, 6.8, false, '{"Stem Cuttings"}', true, NULL, '2026-06-08 04:19:49.742762+00', NULL, 'Average', '{full_sun,part_shade}', 'Perennial', NULL, 'Allow top 2–3 cm of soil to dry between waterings. Keep soil consistently moist but never waterlogged.', 40, 60, 'Intermediate', 'A compact, repeat-flowering garden rose with clusters of small to medium blooms in red, pink, or white, prized for continuous seasonal colour and landscape appeal. Hardy and vigorous with dense foliage.', 'Outdoor', false, false, NULL, true, 'Autumn', true, 'Spring – Autumn', 'Moderate', 'Medium', '{Well-draining,Loamy,Rich}', 'Garden hybrid origin (Rosa multiflora × Rosa × polyantha parentage)', 90, 100, false, 'https://static.inaturalist.org/photos/31170925/square.jpg', 'https://static.inaturalist.org/photos/31170925/medium.jpg', true, 171089, 171089, 'hybrid');
INSERT INTO public.cached_botanical_records (scientific_name, common_name, ideal_min_ph, ideal_max_ph, is_toxic_to_pets, propagation_methods, is_ai_enriched, raw_api_payload, cached_at, toxicity_notes, watering, sunlight, cycle, plant_type, check_depth_description, ideal_humidity_min, ideal_humidity_max, care_difficulty, description, placement, is_tropical, is_toxic_to_humans, human_toxicity_notes, produces_fruit, fruit_season, produces_flowers, flowering_season, growth_rate, maintenance_level, preferred_soil_type, native_region, max_height_cm, max_spread_cm, air_purifying, thumbnail_url, regular_url, thumbnail_fetched, inat_taxon_id, inat_species_id, inat_rank) VALUES ('Phytolacca octandra', 'Inkweed', 6.0, 7.5, true, '{Seeds,Division}', true, NULL, '2026-06-08 04:18:51.8809+00', 'All parts of the plant, especially berries and roots, contain toxic saponins and oxalates. Ingestion causes gastrointestinal irritation, vomiting, and diarrhea in pets.', 'Average', '{full_sun,part_shade}', 'Perennial', NULL, 'Allow top 2–3 cm of soil to dry between waterings; this species tolerates moderate drought once established.', NULL, NULL, 'Beginner', 'A vigorous perennial herb with tall, sturdy stems and elongated leaves, producing clusters of small white flowers followed by distinctive dark purple berries used traditionally for ink and dye production.', 'Outdoor', false, true, 'All parts, especially roots and berries, contain saponins and calcium oxalate crystals. Ingestion causes severe gastrointestinal distress, burning of mouth and throat, and potential systemic toxicity. Skin contact may cause irritation.', true, 'Late Summer – Autumn', true, 'Summer', 'Fast', 'Low', '{Well-draining,Loamy,Rich}', 'South America (Brazil and adjacent regions)', 150, 90, false, 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/17540734/square.jpg', 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/17540734/medium.jpg', true, 155483, 155483, 'species');
