in autumn. Valued as an ornamental shade tree with dramatic seasonal color change and naturally sculptural branching habit.', 'Outdoor', false, false, NULL, true, 'Summer – Autumn', true, 'Spring', 'Moderate', 'Low', '{Well-draining,Loamy,Rich}', 'Pacific Northwest North America (Oregon, Washington, British Columbia)', 1200, 800, false, 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/660331866/square.jpg', 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/660331866/medium.jpg', true, 48227, 48227, 'species');
INSERT INTO public.cached_botanical_records (scientific_name, common_name, ideal_min_ph, ideal_max_ph, is_toxic_to_pets, propagation_methods, is_ai_enriched, raw_api_payload, cached_at, toxicity_notes, watering, sunlight, cycle, plant_type, check_depth_description, ideal_humidity_min, ideal_humidity_max, care_difficulty, description, placement, is_tropical, is_toxic_to_humans, human_toxicity_notes, produces_fruit, fruit_season, produces_flowers, flowering_season, growth_rate, maintenance_level, preferred_soil_type, native_region, max_height_cm, max_spread_cm, air_purifying, thumbnail_url, regular_url, thumbnail_fetched, inat_taxon_id, inat_species_id, inat_rank) VALUES ('Helianthus annuus', 'Common sunflower', 6.0, 7.5, false, '{Seeds}', true, NULL, '2026-05-31 00:46:12.714868+00', NULL, 'Average', '{"full sun"}', 'Annual', 'Annual', 'Check soil 5–7 cm deep; water when dry at this depth. Sunflowers are drought-tolerant once established but require consistent moisture during germination and early growth.', NULL, NULL, 'Beginner', 'A tall, fast-growing annual with large golden-yellow flowers and sturdy green stems. Sunflowers are highly phototropic, tracking the sun during the day, and are widely cultivated for ornamental display, seeds, and oil production.', 'Outdoor', false, false, NULL, true, 'Late Summer – Autumn', true, 'Summer – Early Autumn', 'Fast', 'Low', '{Well-draining,Loamy,Rich}', 'Central and South America', 250, 45, false, 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/323768723/square.jpg', 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/323768723/medium.jpg', true, 57983, 57983, 'species');
INSERT INTO public.cached_botanical_records (scientific_name, common_name, ideal_min_ph, ideal_max_ph, is_toxic_to_pets, propagation_methods, is_ai_enriched, raw_api_payload, cached_at, toxicity_notes, watering, sunlight, cycle, plant_type, check_depth_description, ideal_humidity_min, ideal_humidity_max, care_difficulty, description, placement, is_tropical, is_toxic_to_humans, human_toxicity_notes, produces_fruit, fruit_season, produces_flowers, flowering_season, growth_rate, maintenance_level, preferred_soil_type, native_region, max_height_cm, max_spread_cm, air_purifying, thumbnail_url, regular_url, thumbnail_fetched, inat_taxon_id, inat_species_id, inat_rank) VALUES ('Maianthemum canadense', 'Wild lily of the valley', 4.5, 6.5, true, '{Division,Seeds}', true, NULL, '2026-06-08 15:30:34.325925+00', 'All parts contain cardiac glycosides and saponins; ingestion causes gastrointestinal upset, diarrhea, and potentially cardiac arrhythmias in dogs and cats.', 'Average', '{part_shade,full_shade}', 'Perennial', NULL, 'Keep soil consistently moist but not waterlogged; check at surface and maintain moisture during growing season. Allow slight drying in dormancy.', 50, 75, 'Beginner', 'A delicate woodland perennial with small, heart-shaped leaves and tiny white star-shaped flowers followed by red berries. Native to North American forests, it thrives in shaded, moist conditions and is ideal for naturalising in woodland gardens.', 'Outdoor', false, true, 'All parts contain cardiac glycosides and saponins; ingestion causes gastrointestinal irritation, nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. Skin contact may cause mild dermatitis.', true, 'Late Summer – Autumn', true, 'Late Spring – Early Summer', 'Moderate', 'Low', '{Loamy,Peaty,Moisture-retaining,Well-draining}', 'Eastern North America (Canada and northeastern United States)', 20, 30, false, 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/24815/square.jpg', 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/24815/medium.jpg', true, 67748, 67748, 'species');
INSERT INTO public.cached_botanical_records (scientific_name, common_name, ideal_min_ph, ideal_max_ph, is_toxic_to_pets, propagation_methods, is_ai_enriched, raw_api_payload, cached_at, toxicity_notes, watering, sunlight, cycle, plant_type, check_depth_description, ideal_humidity_min, ideal_humidity_max, care_difficulty, description, placement, is_tropical, is_toxic_to_humans, human_toxicity_notes, produces_fruit, fruit_season, produces_flowers, flowering_season, growth_rate, maintenance_level, preferred_soil_type, native_region, max_height_cm, max_spread_cm, air_purifying, thumbnail_url, regular_url, thumbnail_fetched, inat_taxon_id, inat_species_id, inat_rank) VALUES ('Polygonatum biflorum', 'Solomon''s seal', 6.0, 7.5, true, '{Division,Seeds}', true, NULL, '2026-06-08 15:30:34.29733+00', 'Berries and rhizomes contain cardiac glycosides and saponins; ingestion causes gastrointestinal upset, cardiac arrhythmias, and potential toxicity in pets and humans.', 'Average', '{part_shade,full_shade,filtered_indirect}', 'Perennial', NULL, 'Keep soil consistently moist during active growth (spring–summer); allow soil surface to dry slightly between waterings. In dormancy (autumn–winter), reduce frequency. Check at 2–3 cm depth.', 40, 60, 'Beginner', 'A graceful woodland perennial with arching stems bearing pairs of pendant white flowers and blue-black berries, valued for shade-tolerant foliage and naturalistic garden displays. Highly resilient and low-maintenance once established.', 'Both', false, true, 'Berries and rhizomes contain cardiac glycosides and saponins; ingestion causes severe gastrointestinal upset, cardiac arrhythmias, and systemic toxicity. Skin contact may cause irritation.', true, 'Late Summer – Autumn', true, 'Spring – Early Summer', 'Moderate', 'Low', '{Well-draining,Loamy,Rich,Moisture-retaining}', 'Eastern North America', 90, 60, false, 'https://static.inaturalist.org/photos/38749166/square.jpg', 'https://static.inaturalist.org/photos/38749166/medium.jpg', true, 85327, 85327, 'species');
INSERT INTO public.cached_botanical_records (scientific_name, common_name, ideal_min_ph, ideal_max_ph, is_toxic_to_pets, propagation_methods, is_ai_enriched, raw_api_payload, cached_at, toxicity_notes, watering, sunlight, cycle, plant_type, check_depth_description, ideal_humidity_min, ideal_humidity_max, care_difficulty, description, placement, is_tropical, is_toxic_to_humans, human_toxicity_notes, produces_fruit, fruit_season, produces_flowers, flowering_season, growth_rate, maintenance_level, preferred_soil_type, native_region, max_height_cm, max_spread_cm, air_purifying, thumbnail_url, regular_url, thumbnail_fetched, inat_taxon_id, inat_species_id, inat_rank) VALUES ('Linnaea', 'Twinflowers', 4.5, 6.5, false, '{Seeds,Division}', true, NULL, '2026-06-08 15:02:20.60003+00', NULL, 'Frequent', '{part_shade,filtered_indirect}', 'Perennial', NULL, 'Keep soil consistently moist but not waterlogged; check at surface level regularly during growing season.', 60, 80, 'Advanced', 'A delicate, low-growing boreal perennial with paired pale pink tubular flowers and evergreen foliage, prized by woodland gardeners for its refined beauty and ecological significance as a namesake of Carl Linnaeus.', 'Outdoor', false, false, NULL, true, 'Late Summer', true, 'Late Spring – Early Summer', 'Slow', 'High', '{}', 'Boreal and cool temperate regions of the Northern Hemisphere (North America, Europe, northern Asia)', 15, 30, false, 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/18376/square.jpg', 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/18376/medium.jpg', true, 63137, NULL, 'genus');
INSERT INTO public.cached_botanical_records (scientific_name, common_name, ideal_min_ph, ideal_max_ph, is_toxic_to_pets, propagation_methods, is_ai_enriched, raw_api_payload, cached_at, toxicity_notes, watering, sunlight, cycle, plant_type, check_depth_description, ideal_humidity_min, ideal_humidity_max, care_difficulty, description, placement, is_tropical, is_toxic_to_humans, human_toxicity_notes, produces_fruit, fruit_season, produces_flowers, flowering_season, growth_rate, maintenance_level, preferred_soil_type, native_region, max_height_cm, max_spread_cm, air_purifying, thumbnail_url, regular_url, thumbnail_fetched, inat_taxon_id, inat_species_id, inat_rank) VALUES ('Abies balsamea', 'Balsam fir', 5.0, 6.5, false, '{Seeds}', true, NULL, '2026-06-08 15:02:20.60003+00', NULL, 'Average', '{full_sun,part_shade}', 'Perennial', NULL, 'Keep soil consistently moist but well-draining; check soil moisture at 5 cm depth, ensuring it does not dry completely or become waterlogged.', 40, 60, 'Intermediate', 'A coniferous evergreen tree native to northeastern North America, prized for its pyramidal form, fragrant foliage, and dense branching. Commonly cultivated as an ornamental and Christmas tree, with soft, flattened needles and small upright cones.', 'Outdoor', false, false, NULL, true, 'Autumn', false, NULL, 'Moderate', 'Medium', '{}', 'Northeastern North America, including Eastern Canada and northern United States', 1800, 800, false, 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/9906794/square.jpg', 'https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/9906794/medium.jpg', true, 54261, 54261, 'species');
